WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:09.590
Few things inspire our collective imagination
more than the fragmentary artifacts of ancient

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:14.820
civilizations, ones that once prospered and
are now lost to time.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.360
Looking at objects like these can make you
wonder what other great civilizations could

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.750
have risen and fallen without a trace long
ago.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:28.750
“Fragments of the Past” is a fantasy world
which echoes the lost artifacts and fragmented

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:30.660
tales of classical history.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:36.920
It’s an imaginary bronze age epic — a
vast world inspired by ancient Mediterranean

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:38.550
myth and civilizations.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:45.260
It’s a realm of sun and sea, of gods and
heroes, of war and fine art.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:50.510
The project is the creation of talented Italian
artist and writer Massimiliano Haematinon

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:51.510
Nigro.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:56.070
He’s released an artbook on the epic setting
that much of this video is based on, and I’ve

00:00:56.070 --> 00:01:00.930
included a link in the description — alongside
other ways you can follow and support him.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:05.830
In his book, he depicts imaginary artifacts
from this world — pieces of ancient weapons

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:11.829
and pottery and fragments of poems — alongside
fully realized images of what these civilizations

00:01:11.829 --> 00:01:13.560
might have looked like at their height.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.350
There’s a lot to cover, but I’ll give
you a condensed overview of what’s been

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:18.619
revealed so far.

00:01:18.619 --> 00:01:23.360
I’ve found that exploring the lore of this
world can be a bit like acting as an archeologist

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:29.319
or historian, just of cultures which never
existed — sort of like speculative archeology

00:01:29.319 --> 00:01:33.460
if you will — where one pieces together
artifacts and scattered literature.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:38.259
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
head out into this fictional world, sifting

00:01:38.259 --> 00:01:42.179
through the fragments to see what epic civilizations
might emerge.

00:01:42.179 --> 00:01:44.000
But part of the world building here are the
intentional gaps—of all that is lost to

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.000
time.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.499
The world of “Fragments of the Past” is
set on the massive continent of Askedoria.

00:01:47.499 --> 00:01:54.149
The imagined records suggest the Iskuzai civilization
was one of the most influential in this region.

00:01:54.149 --> 00:02:00.179
According to legend, they began as a nomadic
horde that raged in the steppes beyond the

00:02:00.179 --> 00:02:04.419
mountains, but came to settle in Askedoria
and built a great empire.

00:02:04.419 --> 00:02:09.840
It seems the Iskuzai were a people of contrasts
— who were revered as both fearsome warriors,

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.459
and lovers of poetry and music.

00:02:12.459 --> 00:02:18.220
The artist imagines the Iskuzai’s worshiped
a mysterious and mighty cosmic beast called

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.870
the Double-Crested Panther.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:26.090
Depictions of this deity appear in many Iskuzai
art fragments — and seem to symbolize a

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.730
notion that we might call ‘change.’

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:34.180
When the Iskuzai went to war, or when death
from sickness or injury loomed over a loved

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:37.830
one, they prayed to the Double-Crested Panther
for protection.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:43.010
Only in the most desperate and life-changing
occasions would they ask the help of the Panther.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.959
And they never did so more than three times
a year.

00:02:45.959 --> 00:02:51.099
Believing themselves to be divinely protected,
the Iskuzai seemed at one point to have conquered

00:02:51.099 --> 00:02:56.750
much of Askedoria, amassing a naval fleet
which gave them control over most waterways.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:02.890
With sleek, eel-like boats, the Iskuzai upheld
their expanding empire — which was composed

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:04.989
of various great cities.

00:03:04.989 --> 00:03:10.680
In particular, Beitris was a powerful city-state
during these days — serving as both a wealthy

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.400
coastal port and a stronghold of Iskuzai military
influence.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:20.610
Some of these images reveal the splendor of
Beitris in its prime — and the unique architectural

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:24.000
style of the Iskuzai during this period.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.939
Reading through these fragments, I learned
the second culture that populated Askedoria

00:03:28.939 --> 00:03:31.000
were the Etoi of the coast.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:35.909
A great civilization in their own right, the
Etoi were a developed and architecturally

00:03:35.909 --> 00:03:39.860
distinct group long before the Iskuzai arrived
in Askedoria.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:44.310
Although the Iskuzai took over much Etoi territory
in many fierce battles, in time the Iskuzai

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:47.879
were influenced by the very culture they were
battling.

00:03:47.879 --> 00:03:53.440
Though in many ways invaders, The Iskuzai
found themselves transformed by the civilization

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:59.930
of the Etoi, and began to adopt their sacred
practices like embalming the dead.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:05.560
Most significantly, some fictional artifacts
suggest that the Iskuzai adopted the Etoi

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:11.360
pantheon of Gods — praying to them in situations
that didn’t require the aid of the cosmic

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:12.360
panther.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.720
The Etoi Gods therefore became the most common
on the continent.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:20.860
The author has created art of weathered statues
that depict some of the key deities, like

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:23.800
Phoeraia, the Goddess of the Wild Beasts.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:27.130
Cirta, the Goddess of the underground Waters.

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:29.250
Auleo, God of Knowledge.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:33.780
And Erigonio, the headless God of Music and
Violence.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:39.060
It’s unclear if any of these gods had true
power outside of myth.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:43.720
‘Fragments of the Past’ is a of course
a fantasy story, so perhaps these Gods could

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.600
be ‘real’ within the context of the narrative.

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:51.520
In blurring the distinction between myths
and history, the author highlights how much

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.670
of the past remains a mystery.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:58.210
There are certainly other things about this
fictional world that imply the existence of

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:02.600
the supernatural, particularly when it comes
to Etoi rituals.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:09.200
In some passages, there are rumors of certain
Etoi who had tapped into an unknown ancestral

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.750
power, drawing strength from prophetic dreams.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:17.780
These individuals were revered as oracles,
and conducted processions in the honor of

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:25.280
a forgotten deity: the four-eyed Zagera — the
cyclically-born, the eel ancestor.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.130
Pretty cool, if you ask me.

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:31.780
There are legends that all Etoi people came
from the sea and were exiled from the water

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:35.070
by Zagera for an old, unknown crime.

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:40.060
It’s unclear if there’s any truth to this
creation story, however.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:45.780
Beyond the Etoi and the Iskuzai, I’ve found
whispers throughout the texts of other civilizations,

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:47.360
like the Agrauran.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:53.320
builders of underground catacombs, and the
Kokhlidoi — the worshipers of the stars.

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:57.370
But much of the history of Askedoria has yet
to be uncovered.

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:02.220
So instead, the last finding I’ll touch
on is this figure: Husacci.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:05.830
Perhaps a spirit, perhaps a beast, or perhaps
a little god?

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:12.180
The writings are ambiguous, but no matter
what, Husacci is definitely very old — with

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:17.310
depictions of the creature dating back to
the ancient civilizations of Askedoria.

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:22.160
They’re apparently quite mischievous and
ill-behaved, and summoned by saying their

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:23.350
name three times.

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:27.730
Temperament aside, I’m definitely a fan
of this strange little figure.

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:32.620
There’s plenty more to the lore of “Fragments
of the Past”, and plenty more the author

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:33.990
Massimiliano Haematinon Nigro plans on adding
in the future.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:39.160
Although I don’t think everything will ever
be revealed, as the imagined gaps are part

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:42.850
of what makes the fictional history feel ‘believable.’

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:46.280
If you found the world of ‘Fragments of
the Past’ intriguing, you can check out

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:49.660
the links to the creator’s artbook and social
media below.

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:51.900
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:56.060
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support — and like, subscribe, and hit the

00:06:56.060 --> 00:07:00.100
notification icon to stay up to date on all
things Curious.

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:04.009
See you in the next video, and may the Immortal
Gods shield you from misfortune.

